Lung transplantation is a last-resort procedure for patients with end-stage pulmonary disease. The number performed in the United States has risen over the past decade, but survival still lags behind that of other solid organ transplants.

This paper does two things. First, it trains machine learning models to predict post-transplant survival. Second — and this is the part I find more interesting — it generates counterfactuals: for a given patient, what would have needed to be different for the predicted outcome to change? A risk score tells a clinician where a patient stands. A counterfactual gestures at what, if anything, could be done about it.

This paper results from the work conducted during my Master's thesis, and it represents a continuation of my clinical work with lung transplant recipients, together with my interest in explainability.
